
# High‑Level Project Plan  
**Goal:** Earliest possible working website that can display vaults and published documents, with comments and advanced features added later.

---

# Phase 0 — Foundations (1–2 weeks)

### 0.1 Repository & solution setup
- Create Git repo with `main` and `develop`.
- Add solution structure:
  - `BetaReader.Core`
  - `BetaReader.Interfaces`
  - `BetaReader.Tests`
  - `BetaReader.Service`
  - `BetaReader.Service.Windows`
  - `BetaReader.Wpf`
  - `BetaReader.Website`

### 0.2 Core abstractions (TDD)
Define the minimal interfaces needed for the first vertical slice:
- `IScrivenerAdapter`
- `IWebsiteApi`
- `IFileSystem`
- `IVaultRegistry`
- `ILogger` (stub)
- Domain models: `Vault`, `ScrivenerDocument`, `Snapshot`, `PublishedScene`.

### 0.3 Minimal website skeleton
- Authentication stub (simple login).
- Project list page.
- Document list page.
- Document viewer page (static placeholder).

This gives you a running website before any real data flows.

---

# Phase 1 — First Vertical Slice: Publish snapshots to website (2–3 weeks)

**Objective:**  
A beta reader can log in, see a vault, browse its hierarchy, and read published documents.  
**No comments yet.**

### 1.1 Scrivener → Service snapshot export (Core + Tests)
- Implement eligibility rules (Status threshold + snapshot existence).
- Implement snapshot extraction (fake adapter in tests).
- Implement binder hierarchy mapping.
- TDD:  
  - “Given eligible docs, service exports latest snapshots.”

### 1.2 Service → Website upload
- Define minimal REST endpoints:
  - `POST /vaults/{id}/documents`
  - `POST /vaults/{id}/snapshots`
- Implement `IWebsiteApi` stub + tests.
- Implement sync orchestration (polling loop, idempotency).

### 1.3 Website: real document viewer
- Render uploaded RTF → HTML.
- Show binder hierarchy.
- Show latest snapshot only.

### 1.4 WPF console: vault attach/detach (minimal)
- Attach vault by path.
- Show vault list.
- Show last poll time.

**Deliverable:**  
A working system where you attach a vault, snapshots are published, and the website displays them cleanly.

This is your first “demoable” milestone.

---

# Phase 2 — Reader progress & updated‑scene flags (1–2 weeks)

**Objective:**  
Readers can read scenes, and the system tracks what they’ve read.

### 2.1 Website
- Track last read scene per reader.
- Mark scenes as read.
- Expose “percentage read”.

### 2.2 Service
- Fetch reader progress.
- Calculate percentage read based on binder order.
- Detect when a new snapshot supersedes what a reader has read.
- Mark scenes as “updated”.

### 2.3 UI
- Show “updated” badges.

This gives beta readers a sense of progression and keeps the author aware of what’s been consumed.

---

# Phase 3 — Commenting (Website only) (2–3 weeks)

**Objective:**  
Add commenting to the website, but **do not sync to Scrivener yet**.

### 3.1 Website
- Add comment model.
- Add comment creation UI.
- Add comment threading (one level).
- Store severity/type.
- Display comments in context.

### 3.2 API
- `POST /comments`
- `GET /documents/{id}/comments`

### 3.3 No service integration yet
- Service ignores comments for now.

This gives beta readers a functional feedback tool while keeping the service untouched.

---

# Phase 4 — Comment sync to Scrivener (3–4 weeks)

**Objective:**  
Complete the one‑way loop: Website → Service → Scrivener.

### 4.1 Service
- Poll website for new comments.
- Map website document IDs to Scrivener IDs.
- Write comments into Scrivener as notes.
- Track imported comments to avoid duplication.
- Update website comment status (imported/acknowledged).

### 4.2 Windows notifications
- Show toast notifications for new comments.
- Actions: Acknowledge, Open in Scrivener.

### 4.3 Scrivener automation
- Detect running instance.
- Launch if needed.
- Navigate to correct document.

This is the second major “demoable” milestone: full feedback loop.

---

# Phase 5 — Refinement & WPF diagnostics (2–3 weeks)

### 5.1 WPF console enhancements
- Show pending comments.
- Show number of eligible documents.
- Trigger manual sync.
- Inspect raw data.

### 5.2 Website polish
- Better formatting.
- Improved navigation.
- Reader UX refinements.

### 5.3 Service hardening
- Retry logic.
- Idempotency tests.
- Sync metadata persistence.

---

# Phase 6 — Optional enhancements (ongoing)

- Diffing.
- Analytics.
- Richer comment UI.
- macOS host.
- Logging infrastructure.

---

# Summary Timeline (approximate)

| Phase | Deliverable | Duration |
|------|-------------|----------|
| 0 | Foundations + skeleton website | 1–2 weeks |
| 1 | First vertical slice: publish & read | 2–3 weeks |
| 2 | Reader progress + updated flags | 1–2 weeks |
| 3 | Website commenting | 2–3 weeks |
| 4 | Comment sync + notifications | 3–4 weeks |
| 5 | Diagnostics + polish | 2–3 weeks |

Total initial release: **~10–15 weeks**, depending on pace and depth of testing.

